Ladybird exempts a request whose client is an extension world’s
settings object from the page’s Content Security Policy, in the request
check, the response check, and report-only reporting, so a content
script’s fetch(), XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, and EventSource reach any
host, whatever the page’s connect-src says. Requests of elements the
content script adds to the page stay under the page’s policy.

Blink decides a request’s policy by its world. BaseFetchContext checks
GetContentSecurityPolicyForWorld(options.WorldForCsp()), and for an
isolated world, LocalDOMWindow::GetContentSecurityPolicyForWorld()
returns that world’s own policy. Chrome gives a manifest version 2
extension’s world an empty policy (CSPInfo::GetIsolatedWorldCSP()), and
a version 3 extension’s world one that constrains only script-src and
object-src, so connect-src never binds a content script. XMLHttpRequest
and EventSource record their world when they’re constructed (world_),
FetchManager checks its world_, and WebSocketCommon asks the execution
context’s GetContentSecurityPolicyForCurrentWorld().

WebKit skips the page’s policy when the current world isn’t the normal
one: Document::shouldBypassMainWorldContentSecurityPolicy() looks at the
top call frame’s world, and XMLHttpRequest, EventSource, WebSocket, and
FetchLoader pass ContentSecurityPolicyEnforcement::DoNotEnforce or skip
allowConnectToSource() when it says so.

Gecko lets a content script’s expanded principal overrule the page’s
policy: LoadInfo::GetPolicyContainer() returns the add-on’s policy
container, built from ExpandedPrincipal::GetCsp(), whenever the
triggering principal OverridesCSP() the loading principal, and
nsCSPService::ShouldLoad() checks that one instead of the document’s.
